          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get migraines about eight times a month. Is that the level where guidance describes prevention, and what lifestyle changes are described?",
          "answer": "The retrieved guidance does not say whether eight migraines a month is the level where prevention starts. It also does not describe lifestyle changes. The migraine guidance (National headache society guidance, educational summary) covers when prevention starts only in general terms. It says that overuse of acute medicines is checked before a preventive plan is stepped up. The migraine reference (General clinical reference) defines chronic migraine as headache on 15 or more days a month for more than three months. That is a definition, not a rule for when prevention begins. A clinician can tell you whether preventive treatment makes sense for your pattern.\n\nThe guidance does give limits on acute medicine use. It says to keep treatment days below ten a month for triptans, ergots, opioids and combination pain relievers, and below fifteen a month for simple pain relievers. Frequent use can keep headaches going, so it is worth knowing how often you take medicine for them. A clinician can help you review this.\n\nSeek urgent medical care right away if you have a sudden severe headache that peaks within a minute, a headache with fever and a stiff neck, a headache with new neurological symptoms, or a headache after a head injury. The migraine guidance lists these as emergencies.\n\nQuestions to bring to a clinician: whether your pattern of about eight attacks a month calls for prevention, how often you take acute medicine, and which lifestyle changes might suit you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
